  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Duergar, also known as “Gray Dwarves”, are dwarves who reside in the Underdark. In official DnD canon, they were once slaves to mind flayers, who imbued them with psionic abilities. These abilities are reflected  in the Duergar’s traits as innate spellcasting."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Duergar is the only non-core dwarf subrace in 5th edition, and as of this writing it’s the only dwarf updated to use the post-Tasha’s race design mechanics. Duergar Resilience was replaced with Psionic Resilience, which replaces Advantage on saves against paralysis with Advantage on saves against stun, which makes since because mind flayers and other psychic creatures typically stun things rather than paralyzing them. They also lose the problematic Sunlight Sensitivity, but in exchange the Duergar no longer gets Stonecunning or any of the extra proficiencies that other dwarves get."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Duergar’s innate spellcasting is updated in Monsters of the Multiverse to use the new standard rules (pick your spellcasting ability, re-cast spells using spells slots). The wording isn’t perfectly clear on the Duergar’s weird version, but the community consensus is that you can cast the spells once for free, but only on yourself, and if you use spell slots you can target other creatures."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
